Falsifies `device_tree_blob::verification::be32_reads_big_endian_when_in_bounds`.

The defect: `be32`'s conversion flipped from `from_be_bytes` to `from_le_bytes`. The device tree
blob format is big-endian throughout (the `fdt` spec), and every field this crate hands the
kernel, from the header's own magic number down, goes through this conversion. A byte-swapped
read would still be in range and would still type-check; it just reports the wrong value for
every multi-byte field.

Expected to fail: the harness's own `assert_eq!(v, expected)`, where `expected` is built by hand
as most-significant-byte-first. Run against the whole crate this is the only harness that goes
red: `be32_is_total` and `be64_is_total` assert only that the call returns without panicking, and
`align4_rounds_up_to_a_multiple_of_four` never calls `be32` at all.

diff --git a/crates/device_tree_blob/src/lib.rs b/crates/device_tree_blob/src/lib.rs
index d94af06b1..d9dde952e 100644
--- a/crates/device_tree_blob/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/device_tree_blob/src/lib.rs
@@ -114,7 +114,7 @@ fn be32(bytes: &[u8], at: usize) -> Result<u32, Error> {
     // Proved total in the verification module.
     let end = at.checked_add(4).ok_or(Error::Truncated)?;
     let slice = bytes.get(at..end).ok_or(Error::Truncated)?;
-    Ok(u32::from_be_bytes(slice.try_into().unwrap()))
+    Ok(u32::from_le_bytes(slice.try_into().unwrap()))
 }

